@startuml
title MIDI at 31.25 kbaud — the message layer and the counted cell that carries it
' Sources: firmware/midi_xfer.pe (the message layer, sbit/sb_start/sb_stop,
'          sb_hold's 13-clock body x 146), tb/tb_pe_soc_midi.v (the
'          oversampling receiver, the per-frame rate measurement),
'          reviews/2026-09-25/FW-BUS-BLOCK3-FINDINGS.md (the measured numbers)
' Wire: 90 20 40 21 41 22 42 80 23 43 24 44 25 45
'       6 messages, 2 status bytes, 14 bytes - not 18
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam state {
  FontSize 11
  BackgroundColor<<msg>>  #fbe9cf
  BorderColor<<msg>>      #b8813a
  FontColor<<msg>>        #5a3d12
  BackgroundColor<<stat>> #dce9f8
  BorderColor<<stat>>     #4a6fa5
  FontColor<<stat>>       #16324f
  BackgroundColor<<data>> #dceee0
  BorderColor<<data>>     #4a8a5c
  FontColor<<data>>       #17452a
  BackgroundColor<<box>>  #f2f2f2
  BorderColor<<box>>      #808080
  FontColor<<box>>        #303030
}

state "MESSAGE LAYER — decides WHAT goes on the wire" as msg <<box>> {
  state "main\nLDM A,1 (the message counter m)\nSUB A,6 / JZ done" as m0 <<box>>
  state "is a status byte due?\nLDM A,2 (the mod-3 counter)\nJNZ go_note\nthe ISA has neither a\ndivision nor a modulo, and\nthat is why DECX exists" as m1 <<box>>
  state "stat_on / stat_go\nm = 0 -> 0x90  note on\nm = 3 -> 0x80  note off\ndmem[3] = the status byte\ndmem[5]++ HERE, not in the\ntransmitter: it cannot tell a\nstatus byte from a data byte\nand must not guess" as m2 <<stat>>
  state "go_note\nnote = 0x20 + m\ndmem[13] = 2 (velocity follows)" as m3 <<data>>
  state "go_vel\nvelocity = 0x40 + m\ndmem[13] = 0 (message complete)" as m4 <<data>>
  state "next_msg\ndmem[2] + 1, SUB A,3 / JZ mod3_zero\nthen dmem[1]++" as m5 <<box>>
  state "done\ndmem[11] = 0xA5" as m6
  m0 --> m1
  m1 --> m2
  m1 --> m3
  m2 --> m5 : after the status byte
  m3 --> m5 : after the note
  m4 --> m5
  m5 --> m0
  m0 --> m6 : m == 6
}

state "TRANSMITTER — one loop over the ten bit cells of a frame\nno CALL/RET in this ISA, so a shared cell routine would need\na return address in a dmem slot and a dispatch to get back;\nten cells in a loop is also how the wire is actually shaped" as tx <<box>> {
  state "sbit — the cell dispatch\nLDM A,7 / SUB A,9 / JZ sb_stop\nADD A,9 / JZ sb_start\n(8-bit arithmetic wraps, so\nthat ADD is the index again)" as t0 <<box>>
  state "the DATA branch\nLDM A,0 / AND A,1 / OUT TXPIN\nLSB FIRST\nthen LDM/SHR/STM: THE SHIFT\nLIVES HERE AND NOT IN THE\nCOMMON TAIL - a cell that\ncarries no data bit must not\nconsume one" as t1 <<data>>
  state "sb_start\nLDI A,0 / OUT TXPIN\n3 NOPs: this dispatch to a data\ncell is 2 instructions longer\nthan a data cell's own head,\nand they make up the difference" as t2 <<stat>>
  state "sb_stop\n3 NOPs, then LDI A,1 / OUT\n5 more NOPs, AFTER the output:\nthe stop cell's own length is the\npath from its output to the NEXT\nframe's start bit" as t3 <<stat>>
  state "sb_hold — THE PIECE THIS ACT EXISTS FOR\n11 NOPs + SUB A,1 + JNZ = a 13-clock body,\n146 iterations.  21 + 1 + 146 x 13 = 1920 clocks\n= 32.000 us = exactly 31,250 baud" as t4 <<box>>
  t0 --> t1
  t0 --> t2
  t0 --> t3
  t1 --> t4
  t2 --> t4
  t3 --> t4
  t4 --> t0 : dmem[6]--, dmem[7]++\nthen return via dmem[13]
}

state "THE RETURN POINT — dmem[13]\n0 = the status byte, so next_msg\n1 = go_note, so the note follows\n2 = go_vel, so the message is done\nthe ISA has no CALL/RET, so a shared\ntransmitter gets an explicit\ncontinuation rather than being written\nout three times" as ret <<box>>

[*] --> msg
msg --> tx : JMP send_byte
tx --> ret
ret --> msg

note right of t4
  A CELL'S LENGTH BELONGS TO THE PATH
  BETWEEN TWO CELLS. The time from one
  cell's OUTPUT to the next cell's
  OUTPUT depends on WHICH CELL IS NEXT,
  because the dispatch that selects the
  next cell's code is only partly
  executed on each path. So the eleven
  NOPs above exist only to make some
  OTHER cell come out at 1920. Measured
  at clock resolution, one frame showed
  cells of 1921, 1920 and 1917 — a
  receiver tolerates that and an
  assertion about the rate does not.
end note
note right of t1
  THE THREE DEFECTS THIS PROGRAM HAD,
  none of which was found by reading
  the code:
   1 the rate was TWICE MIDI's (a cell
     is a whole bit, not a half: 16.316
     us measured = 61,286 baud)
   2 every byte lost its two lowest bits
     (the shift was in the common tail,
     so the start and stop cells each
     shifted: 0x90 went out as 0x24)
   3 there was no stop bit at the end of
     the frame, and the receiver decoded
     THIRTEEN OF FOURTEEN bytes correctly
     because a back-to-back stream's
     missing stop bit is the next frame's
     first cell. Only the last frame
     failed, on a single stop sample.
end note
@enduml

@startuml
title MIDI — one byte is ten cells, and a message is three of them (or one and a quarter)
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #eef3fb
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #4a6fa5
skinparam ParticipantFontColor #16324f
skinparam SequenceGroupBackgroundColor #fbfbfb
skinparam SequenceGroupBorderColor #b0b0b0
skinparam ArrowColor #333333

participant "start" as st
participant "d0" as d0
participant "d1" as d1
participant "d2" as d2
participant "d3" as d3
participant "d4" as d4
participant "d5" as d5
participant "d6" as d6
participant "d7" as d7
participant "stop" as sp
participant "next" as nx

note over st, nx
  shown: 0x90, the note-on status byte, sent once at m = 0.
  Every cell is 1920 clocks = 32.000 us, so a byte is
  320.000 us and the 31.25 kbaud wire carries
  3,125 bytes per second.
end note
st -> d0
d0 -> d1
d1 -> d2
d2 -> d3
d3 -> d4
d4 -> d5
d5 -> d6
d6 -> d7
d7 -> sp
sp -> nx : back to back: the next frame's start bit
note over d0, d7
  LSB FIRST, so d0 is bit 0. 0x90 = 1001 0000:
  the status byte is two nibbles - a
  message type and a channel - and the
  top bit being set is what makes the
  wire self-delimiting: a receiver
  decides "status or data" from the top
  bit alone.
end note

@enduml

@startuml
title MIDI running status — six messages, two status bytes, fourteen bytes
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #eef3fb
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #4a6fa5
skinparam ParticipantFontColor #16324f
skinparam ActorBackgroundColor #fbe9cf
skinparam ActorBorderColor #b8813a
skinparam ActorFontColor #5a3d12
skinparam SequenceGroupBackgroundColor #fbfbfb
skinparam SequenceGroupBorderColor #b0b0b0
skinparam ArrowColor #333333

actor "master" as M
participant "wire" as W
actor "receiver\nimplements the rule" as R

group m = 0  note on — the status byte IS sent
  M -> W : 90
  W -> R : status 0x90 REPLACES the running status
  M -> W : 20
  M -> W : 40
  R -> R : message 0: 90 20 40
end
group m = 1, 2  running status 0x90
  M -> W : 21
  M -> W : 41
  R -> R : message 1: under 0x90
  M -> W : 22
  M -> W : 42
  R -> R : message 2: under 0x90
end
group m = 3  a NEW status byte: the direction flips
  M -> W : 80
  W -> R : 0x80 REPLACES 0x90, it does not add to it
  M -> W : 23
  M -> W : 43
  R -> R : message 3: note OFF
end
group m = 4, 5  running status 0x80
  M -> W : 24
  M -> W : 44
  M -> W : 25
  M -> W : 45
  R -> R : messages 4 and 5: note OFF
end

note over M, R
  SIX MESSAGES COST EIGHTEEN BYTES if every one repeats its
  status byte, and fourteen with running status. The saving is the
  point of the feature: a 31.25 kbaud wire saturates near 3,900
  bytes per second and the status bytes are a fifth of them.

  THE PART THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG, and which the TB checks: a
  receiver that KEPT 0x90 after seeing 0x80 would decode messages
  4 and 5 as note-ONS - six messages, the right notes and
  velocities, and the wrong direction on half of them. The
  firmware sends 0x80 exactly once, at m = 3, and the TB's expected
  list puts messages 4-6 under 0x80.

  AND THE SAVING IS ASSERTED, NOT INFERRED: a firmware that
  repeated the status byte would still decode into the right six
  messages under a receiver that ignored running status, so the
  byte count is checked directly - exactly 2 status bytes and
  exactly 14 bytes on the wire, against 6 messages.
end note
@enduml

@startuml
!pragma teoz
title MIDI — one frame of 0x90, ten cells of 1920 clocks = 32.000 us each; the axis is 4 units per cell
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
robust "branch" as F
concise "TX pin" as T
concise "cells" as N
@0
F is "cell 0 start"
T is 0
N is "start"
@4
F is "cells 1-7 data"
T is 0
N is "d0 = 0"
@8
T is 0
N is "d1 = 0"
@12
T is 0
N is "d2 = 0"
@16
T is 0
N is "d3 = 0"
@20
T is 1
N is "d4 = 1"
@24
T is 0
N is "d5 = 0"
@28
T is 0
N is "d6 = 0"
@32
T is 1
N is "d7 = 1"
@36
F is "cell 9 stop"
T is 1
N is "stop"
@40
F is "next frame"
T is 0
N is "back to back"
@48
N is "1920 clks"
@56
N is "32.000 us"
@64
N is "measured 31.9987"
@endsync
@enduml

